# MAGIC <div class="hero"><div class="blob b1"></div><div class="blob b2"></div><div class="blob b3"></div><div class="hero-in"><div class="kicker">Advanced Data Engineering with Databricks</div><h1><span class="kicker-inline">Recap</span>Automated Deployment with Databricks Asset Bundles</h1><div class="chips"><span class="chip">Course 4 of 4</span><span class="chip">⏱ ~9 minutes</span></div><p class="sub">Describing jobs, pipelines and code <strong>as YAML in Git</strong>, and deploying the <strong>same project</strong> to dev, test and prod with the Databricks CLI and a CI/CD pipeline. (Bundles are now also called <em>Declarative Automation Bundles</em>.)</p></div></div><div class="idea"><span class="idea-icon">💡</span><div><b>The one idea to remember:</b> one bundle, many <strong>targets</strong>. The code and resource definitions never change between environments, only <strong>variables and target overrides</strong> do. CI runs <code>validate</code> → <code>deploy</code> → <code>run</code> for you.</div></div>
